Q: Why doesn’t margin: auto center my fixed-width &lt;div&gt; inside a flex container? A: <p>Try using align-items and justify-content: center;  to Center your Div.</p>
<p>There are three properties for these elements though, Align-self/items/content. the same goes for justify.</p>
<p>that works for centering divs. if you want to center text inside the block, using text-align works. Text-align: center;</p>
<p>justify for vertical centering (my bad).</p>
<hr />
<p>something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.parent {

display: flex;

flex-direction: column;

justify-content: center;

align-items: center;

}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>column makes the entire div face vertically through the page, like two Tetris blocks on top of each other.</p>
<p>Row makes the entire div face horizontally. Like a train and its carriage ways.</p>
<p>The roles of justify-content and align-items depend on your flex-direction.</p>
<p>Flex-direction: row; Justify-content moves horizontally; align-items moves vertically.</p>
<p>with flex-direction: column; justify-content moves vertically and align items move horizontally.</p>
<p>Almost forgot. HTML structure matters, when using flexbox , make sure to add that element in the parent container. As flex only affects direct child containers. (You can think of that as an onion)</p>
<hr />
<p>e.g.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;parent&quot;&gt;
  &lt;section class=&quot;child&quot;&gt; 

    &lt;h1&gt; hello world &lt;/h1&gt;

 &lt;/section&gt;
&lt;/div&gt; 
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.parent {

display: flex;

flex-direction: column;

justify-content: center;

align-items: center;

}

.child {

color: blue;

font-size: 0.5rem;

}
</code></pre>
<p>make sure your flex element is in the parent container,</p>
<p>P.S. I was wrong about Justify-items, that is an element only used in grid layouts.</p>
<p>But yeah, best of luck.</p>
<p>If any of this information is wrong, lemme know.</p>

Q: UI Freezing when passing prop function from parent to child component A: <p>Your issue is caused by parent rerendering and in turn, child component rerenders too. And those rerenders happen in your code because <code>message</code> state updates from children happen on every click.</p>
<p>In order to prevent rerendering children component, you need to memoize the component, as it updates parent state, but child component does not change due to that. So we can use <code>React.memo</code>.</p>
<p>But now we need to also &quot;stabilize&quot; the prop passed to children component. This can be done with <code>useCallback</code> hook.</p>
<p>In order to fix this you should:</p>
<ul>
<li>Memoize the callback in the parent using <code>useCallback</code></li>
<li>Memoize the child component using <code>React.memo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Below is simplified setup of your components:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const Support = () =&gt; {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(null);

  const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{message} : Data from Child&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;GridData handleChildData={handleChildData} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
};

const GridData = React.memo(({ handleChildData }) =&gt; {
  const table = useMaterialReactTable(
    useMemo(() =&gt; ({
      data,
      columns,
      muiTableBodyRowProps: ({ row }) =&gt; ({
        onClick: () =&gt; handleChildData(row.original.name),
        sx: { cursor: 'pointer' },
      }),
    }), [data, columns, handleChildData])
  );

  return &lt;MaterialReactTable table={table} /&gt;;
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>BONUS</strong></p>
<p>Setters returned from <code>useState</code> hook are stable, so you can pass <code>setMesage</code> function and omit entirely</p>
<pre><code>const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
}, []);
</code></pre>
<p>and then pass <code>setMessage</code> directly</p>
<pre><code>&lt;GridData handleChildData={setMessage} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>It will keep working the same :)</p>
